{
  "title": "Parser: split the remaining notes and amounts out of item, then re-run make catalog-clean-aliases",
  "description": "The 2026-09-29 parser fix (peeling every trailing comma note, trailing usage phrases, leading \"about\", checkbox bullets) let make catalog-clean-aliases drop 563 curated alias keys and re-key 74. \
About 300 alias keys still look like parse junk because the parser leaves text in item. \
Remaining patterns: a leading article plus size (\"a 3-inch piece of ginger\"); \"x or 2 slim ones\" style alternatives with an amount; \"zest from 1 lemon\" and \"cornstarch mixed with 1 tablespoon water\" (no comma); comma chains whose rightmost part the prep detector doesn't know, so earlier parts never get peeled (\"crisp, dry white wine, doesn't have to be fancy\"); a colon label (\"egg wash: 1 large egg beaten with 1 tablespoon milk\"). \
Fix the parser for the common ones, run make ingredient-tests-update, make catalog-clean-aliases and make pipeline, and review logs/catalog-alias-cleanup.md. \
That report also lists 139 conflicts: keys whose parsed name resolves differently, or where moving the key would drop an example, an alternative or an amount, or would broaden an ambiguous alias (for example \"chickpeas, rinsed\" maps to drained chickpeas while \"chickpeas\" means canned solids and liquids, and \"vegetable, canola, or olive oil\" is ambiguous while its junk variants map to vegetable oil). \
Each needs a person to pick the right mapping for the clean name, after which the junk key can go.",
